{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgS5tCz9rwLYp9DLwgDQS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I fixed the integration. There were three separate bugs, and each one alone would break it. I wrote the causes, fixes and notes on the colleague's write-up in `ANSWER.md`.\n\n1. **The Connect button got 401 for everyone.** It was a plain link to `GET /api/intervals/auth/init`. When the browser follows a link, it doesn't send the `Authorization` header the app keeps in `localStorage`. That endpoint is now a POST that returns `{authorize_url}`, and the button calls it with `fetch` and then goes to that URL.\n2. **Intervals rejected the authorize step with `400 invalid_request`.** Since the calendar sync shipped on 10-02, the scope named ACTIVITY and CALENDAR twice each (`:READ` and `:WRITE`), and Intervals rejects a repeated area. The scope is now `ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:READ,CALENDAR:WRITE`.\n3. **Rivera was marked expired after an hour.** Intervals tokens never expire and have no refresh token, but the code assumed a 3600-second lifetime, so the background refresher deleted a valid token. I removed the made-up lifetime and the refresher. The app now treats a connection as revoked only when Intervals actually returns 401, and then shows \"revoked, reconnect\".\n\nI tested the whole flow against a fake Intervals server I ran locally: init, callback, wellness data, and the 401 after a revoke all behave as expected. I haven't tried it against real Intervals.icu, since I had no credentials.\n\nFrom the colleague's note, the parts on the POST init, the duplicate scope and \"no refresh token\" all applied. I kept our own scope list rather than theirs, which adds a `SETTINGS:WRITE` this app doesn't need. Their advice to drop `grant_type` and `redirect_uri` from the token exchange didn't apply: Rivera's exchange worked with them, so I left it alone.\n\nTwo things for you:\n- **Rivera must click Connect once more.** The old refresher already deleted their token.\n- **Restart the server on port 8766.** A `server.py` was already running there, probably the old code. I didn't stop it; I tested on port 8767 instead."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":103,"cache_read_input_tokens":41532,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":103},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a10edcbb-e369-4f07-9ef6-d44febd921e1","uuid":"48069198-6638-48df-913b-2a7dbe52c294","timestamp":"2026-10-27T04:08:54.254Z","request_id":"req_011CgS5tCk26fzkeUkRe8Wtx"}
